---
// web/src/pages/legal/terms/index.astro
// /legal/terms/, the terms of use (Phase 5 Step 16; ROADMAP §4 5.7; ADR-0013
// decision 20): the service and its licence, acceptable use and the API's
// rate limits, the data's own terms with the New York Fed's and the OECD's
// conditions passed through as their terms require, every source this site
// serves linked to its terms (generated from sources.json), the FRED® API's
// terms for anyone reading FRED through pyeconomics, no warranty, a limit of
// liability, changes, the governing law and the contact. The New York Fed's
// and the OECD's words are quoted from their terms as read on 2026-11-12, and
// the questions still open for counsel are marked (CounselNote).
import Breadcrumbs from "@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import CounselNote from "@/components/CounselNote.astro";
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import {
  CONTACT_EMAIL,
  FRED_API_TERMS,
  GOVERNING_LAW,
  LEGAL_INDEX,
  NYFED_TERMS,
  OECD_TERMS,
  OPERATOR,
  RATE_LIMITS,
  REPOSITORY,
  TERMS,
} from "@/lib/legal";
import { sourceData } from "@/lib/site-data";
import { webPage, type Crumb } from "@/lib/structured-data";

const trail: Crumb[] = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: LEGAL_INDEX.title, path: LEGAL_INDEX.path },
  { name: TERMS.title, path: TERMS.path },
];
const served = sourceData.filter((source) => source.snapshot_series > 0);
const h2 = "mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold";
const p = "mt-3 max-w-3xl";
const quote = "mt-3 max-w-3xl border-l-4 pl-4";
---

<Base
  title={TERMS.title}
  description={TERMS.description}
  jsonLd={webPage(TERMS.path, TERMS.title, TERMS.description, trail)}
  searchable
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={trail} />
  <span hidden data-pagefind-meta="kind:Legal"></span>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">{TERMS.title}</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Last updated {TERMS.updated}</p>

  <h2 id="about" class={h2}>1. About these terms</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    These terms govern your use of pyeconomics.dev, its API under <code>/v1/</code> and its MCP endpoint at
    <code>/mcp</code> (together, "the service"), which {OPERATOR} ("we", "us") operates. By using the service you accept
    these terms; if you do not accept them, do not use it. The <a href="/legal/privacy/">privacy policy</a> and the
    <a href="/legal/disclaimers/">disclaimers</a> form part of them.
  </p>
  <p class={p}>
    The pyeconomics software you install yourself (the Python package, the command line, the local app and the MCP
    server you run) is governed by its licence, not by these terms, except that the data it reads stays subject to each
    source's terms (section 4).
  </p>

  <h2 id="licence" class={h2}>2. The software and its licence</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    pyeconomics is open-source software under the <a href="/legal/licence/">Apache License, Version 2.0</a>. Its
    source, this site's included, is public at <a href={REPOSITORY}>{REPOSITORY}</a>. The licence's terms, its
    disclaimer of warranty and its limitation of liability among them, apply to the software whichever way you reach
    it. These terms add nothing to what the licence lets you do with the software.
  </p>

  <h2 id="acceptable-use" class={h2}>3. Acceptable use and rate limits</h2>
  <p class={p}>The service is free. Use it lawfully and fairly. Do not:</p>
  <ul class="mt-2 max-w-3xl list-disc pl-6">
    <li>try to get around its security, its access controls or its rate limits, or test its security without following the <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>security policy</a>;</li>
    <li>send requests meant to overload it or to run up its costs;</li>
    <li>use it to copy a data source's own product, or to download a source's data in bulk to redistribute it;</li>
    <li>use it in a way that breaks the law or a data source's terms; or</li>
    <li>say or imply that we, or any data source, endorse you, your product or your use of the service.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class={p}>
    The API limits each client's requests over a moving minute: {RATE_LIMITS.runs.replace("/", " a ")} model runs,
    {" "}{RATE_LIMITS.series.replace("/", " a ")} series reads and {RATE_LIMITS.metadata.replace("/", " a ")} other
    requests. Beyond a limit it answers <code>429</code> with a <code>Retry-After</code> header, and a costly run has
    budgets of its own. The limits can change. For heavy use, run pyeconomics on your own computer, where the only limits
    are the data sources' own.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data" class={h2}>4. The data and its terms</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    The data the models read is not ours: each source publishes it under its own terms, and those terms stay with it
    wherever it goes. This site serves only data whose source's terms allow a third-party service to display it, from a
    daily snapshot taken from the body that publishes it. Every chart, table and file carries the source's attribution
    and required notices. Keep them when you reuse the data, and follow the source's terms, which may limit what you
    can do with it. <a href="/sources/">Data sources and licences</a> lists every source, its class and its notices.
  </p>

  <h3 id="nyfed" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">4.1 Data from the Federal Reserve Bank of New York</h3>
  <p class={p}>
    Content from the Federal Reserve Bank of New York is made available to you with the same permissions, conditions
    and restrictions set forth in the <a href={NYFED_TERMS}>New York Fed's Terms of Use</a>; these terms add none that
    are more restrictive. Among them, in the New York Fed's words:
  </p>
  <blockquote class={quote} data-pass-through="nyfed">
    <p>
      "If you distribute the Content, you must make the Content available with the same permissions, conditions, and
      restrictions set forth in these Terms. You may not impose more restrictive terms or conditions on the Content."
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      "When you copy or distribute any Content, you must include any copyright notice and other source identifiers that
      the New York Fed includes with that Content."
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      "You must not state or imply that the New York Fed endorses your use, reproduction, or distribution of the Content
      or any product, service, financial instrument, or material you create or derive using the Content or any excerpt
      of the Content."
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      "If you modify any of the Content, you must clearly label the modified Content to ensure there is no confusion or
      ambiguity about the difference between the original Content and the modifications or the source of the
      modifications. You may not attribute any modifications or derivative works to the New York Fed."
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">"None of the Content is investment advice."</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p class={p}>
    pyeconomics modifies the New York Fed's content in one way: rates published as percentages are shown as decimals
    (5.31% as 0.0531). The modification is ours, not the New York Fed's. Each reference rate (SOFR, BGCR, TGCR, EFFR
    and OBFR) carries the notice the Terms of Use require, for example:
  </p>
  <blockquote class={quote}>
    <p>
      "The SOFR is subject to the Terms of Use posted at newyorkfed.org. The New York Fed is not responsible for
      publication of the SOFR by pyeconomics, does not sanction or endorse any particular republication, and has no
      liability for your use."
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      "The Secured Overnight Financing Rate (SOFR) Data and Broad General Collateral Rate (BGCR) Data are calculated
      using data provided under a license granted to the New York Fed by DTCC Solutions LLC (“Solutions”), an affiliate
      of The Depository Trust &amp; Clearing Corporation. Solutions, its affiliates, and third parties from which they
      obtained data have no liability for the content of this material."
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p class={p}>
    pyeconomics is not affiliated with the New York Fed. The New York Fed does not sanction, endorse, or recommend any
    products or services offered by pyeconomics.
  </p>

  <h3 id="oecd" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">4.2 Data from the OECD</h3>
  <p class={p}>
    Data from the OECD is subject to the <a href={OECD_TERMS}>OECD's terms and conditions</a>, which say:
  </p>
  <blockquote class={quote} data-pass-through="oecd">
    <p>
      "Except where additional restrictions apply as stated above, you can extract from, download, copy, adapt, print,
      distribute, share and embed Data for any purpose, even for commercial use. You must give appropriate credit to
      the OECD by using the citation associated with the relevant Data, or, if no specific citation is available, you
      must cite the source information using the following format: OECD (year), (dataset name),(data source) DOI or URL
      (accessed on (date)). When sharing or licensing work created using the Data, you agree to include the same
      acknowledgment requirement in any sub-licenses that you grant, along with the requirement that any further
      sub-licensees do the same."
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      "Data may be subject to restrictions beyond the scope of these Terms and Conditions, either because specific terms
      apply to those Data or because third parties may have ownership interests."
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p class={p}>
    So when you use OECD data from pyeconomics, cite it as the OECD asks, and require the same of anyone you pass it on
    to, and of anyone they pass it on to. Check a dataset's own metadata for third-party restrictions before you reuse
    it. The OECD does not endorse pyeconomics or your use of its data, and provides the data "as is"; it is without
    prejudice to the status of or sovereignty over any territory.
  </p>

  <h3 id="fred" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">4.3 FRED® data on your own computer</h3>
  <p class={p}>
    This site never serves FRED® or ALFRED® data. When you use pyeconomics on your own computer to read them, with your
    own key, you agree to be bound by the <a href={FRED_API_TERMS}>FRED® API Terms of Use</a>, and what pyeconomics
    shows you from FRED carries the notice those terms require. FRED's terms forbid storing or caching its data, so
    pyeconomics never writes it to disk.
  </p>

  <h3 id="hosted-sources" class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">4.4 Every source this site serves</h3>
  <ul class="mt-3 max-w-3xl list-disc pl-6" data-hosted-terms="">
    {
      served.map((source) => (
        <li>
          <a href={source.terms_url} rel="noopener noreferrer">
            {source.name}
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2 id="not-advice" class={h2}>5. Not advice</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    The service is for education and information. It gives no investment, financial, tax, legal or accounting advice,
    and nothing it returns is a recommendation. Read the <a href="/legal/disclaimers/">disclaimers</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="no-warranty" class={h2}>6. No warranty</h2>
  <p class={`${p} uppercase`}>
    The service, its results and its data are provided "as is" and "as available", without warranty of any kind,
    express or implied, including any warranty of accuracy, completeness, timeliness, merchantability, fitness for a
    particular purpose or non-infringement. We do not warrant that the service will be uninterrupted, error-free or
    secure, or that its data is current or correct.
  </p>

  <h2 id="liability" class={h2}>7. Limitation of liability</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    To the fullest extent the law allows, we are not liable for any indirect, incidental, special, consequential or
    punitive damages, or for any loss of profits, revenue, data or opportunity, arising from your use of the service,
    its results or its data, even if we were told they were possible. Our total liability for all claims about the
    service is limited to one hundred US dollars (US$100). Some laws do not allow these limits; where they do not, the
    limits apply only as far as the law allows.
  </p>
  <CounselNote question="the amount of the cap, and whether the limits hold for readers in the EU and the UK." />

  <h2 id="changes" class={h2}>8. Changes</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    We may change, suspend or end the service, or any part of it, at any time. We may change these terms; the date at
    the top says when they last changed, and the repository keeps every earlier version. Using the service after a
    change means you accept the new terms.
  </p>

  <h2 id="law" class={h2}>9. Governing law</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    These terms are governed by the laws of {GOVERNING_LAW}, without regard to its conflict-of-laws rules. Nothing in
    them takes away rights the law where you live gives you and that cannot be given up by contract.
  </p>
  <CounselNote question="the governing law, and where a dispute is heard." />

  <h2 id="contact" class={h2}>10. Contact</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Write to {OPERATOR} at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. Report a vulnerability privately
    through the <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>security policy</a>, never by email or a public issue.
  </p>
</Base>
